//? Title: Isomap: Neighbour Graphs, Geodesics and Shortcuts
//? Subtitle: Hand-checkable paths and spectra, Swiss-roll recovery and an iris connectivity study with datamunge
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
High-dimensional observations can lie on a curved surface where straight-line distance
crosses folds rather than following the surface. We want low-dimensional coordinates
that preserve travel distances along that surface, while recognizing when the sampled
points do not determine those distances reliably. Isomap replaces global straight-line
distances by shortest paths through local neighbours, then applies classical scaling.
We work through a four-point line, a square cycle and two disconnected triangles before
checking a Swiss roll with known intrinsic coordinates and Fisher's iris measurements.
The square has perfect positive-inertia fit and zero residual variance yet distorted
edge lengths. On a 1000-point roll, six neighbours give a 7.45% intrinsic distance error,
while forty give 67.53%. Iris needs 25 neighbours for connectivity under the stated graph
rule; that connection threshold is not evidence that the flowers form one sampled manifold.
)

\toc

> The method

>> Local measurements define a graph

Let \(x_i\in\mathbb R^p\), \(i=1,\ldots,n\), be the retained feature rows.
`dm::stats::Isomap` uses raw Euclidean or Manhattan distances. Feature units and scaling
therefore define the model; standardizing a column changes neighbourhoods and paths,
not just the axis labels. This report uses Euclidean distances throughout.

For each point retain exactly \(k\) other neighbours. Equal distances are ordered by
increasing retained-row index, making the choice reproducible. The undirected graph uses
the *union* rule: an edge exists if either endpoint selects the other. It is not the
mutual-neighbour graph, so a vertex can have degree greater than \(k\). An edge has
its original feature distance as weight. Duplicate observations retain their zero-length
edges; a zero weight does not mean an edge is absent.

`isomap_graph()` exposes the directed selections, union adjacency, original distances,
component labels and retained row indices without requiring connectivity. Formula-free
numeric features are selected explicitly, nulls are omitted jointly and retained
nonfinite values are rejected. Labels and synthetic truth columns are never included
unless explicitly named as features. The current API requires \(2\le k<n\).

>> Shortest paths estimate geodesics

Starting with edge weights, zeros on the diagonal and infinity for absent edges,
Floyd–Warshall applies

$$
 D_{ij}^{(m)}=\min\{D_{ij}^{(m-1)},D_{im}^{(m-1)}+D_{mj}^{(m-1)}\}.
$$
\alttext(At step m, the shortest path from i to j either avoids vertex m or joins a shortest i-to-m path to a shortest m-to-j path.)

Induction on the allowed intermediate vertices proves that the final \(D_G\) contains
all-pairs shortest-path distances. A disconnected graph has infinite cross-component
distances, so no single finite classical-scaling matrix exists. The constructor rejects
it; it does not invent inter-component edges. Inspect the graph or fit components
separately before deciding whether a larger neighbourhood makes scientific sense.
Some other implementations, including scikit-learn, repair disconnected graphs, so their
results need not have this same meaning.

For Euclidean input edges, the triangle inequality gives \(d_E(i,j)\le D_G(i,j)\).
There is no universal ordering between \(D_G\) and the true manifold distance:
sparse paths can detour, while chord edges and cross-fold shortcuts can underestimate it.
The original consistency argument requires suitable sampling density, small local
neighbourhoods, controlled curvature/branch separation and an intrinsically Euclidean
geometry. It does not say that a connected graph or an attractive plot certifies recovery.
A circle or a sheet with holes need not have a globally distance-preserving flat map.

With fixed distance ties, increasing \(k\) only adds edges. Component counts and shortest
paths can therefore only decrease. That monotonicity does not make embedding quality
monotone. At \(k=n-1\), every pair is an edge and \(D_G=d_E\); Isomap reduces to
ordinary Euclidean classical MDS, which has the same centred score geometry as unscaled PCA.

>> Classical scaling and what its spectrum means

Let \(J=I-\mathbf1\mathbf1^\top/n\). Squaring distances elementwise and centring gives

$$
 B=-\tfrac12 J D_G^{\circ2}J=V\Lambda V^\top,\qquad
 Y_d=V_{1:d}\operatorname{diag}\{\sqrt{\max(\lambda_1,0)},\ldots,
                                      \sqrt{\max(\lambda_d,0)}\}.
$$
\alttext(The centred Gram matrix is minus one half the doubly centred squared graph-distance matrix. Coordinates use its largest eigenvectors scaled by square roots of positive eigenvalues; negative modes contribute zero.)

If \(D_G\) is Euclidean, \(B\) is positive semidefinite and sufficiently many axes
recover its distances. A general graph metric can produce negative eigenvalues. Orthogonal
invariance of the Frobenius norm shows that dropping all negative modes and retaining
the largest positive modes minimizes \(\|B-YY^\top\|_F\) among rank-at-most-\(d\)
positive-semidefinite Gram matrices. This is a *strain* optimum, not a least-squares
distance-stress optimum. The full signed spectrum is available from `spectrum()`.
Eigenvector signs and rotations within repeated eigenspaces are arbitrary; axis-by-axis
correlations are therefore a poor general comparison between implementations.

The historical `goodness_of_fit()` is only
\(\sum_{a\le d}\max(\lambda_a,0)/\sum_a\max(\lambda_a,0)\). It omits negative modes.
`dimension_diagnostics()` separately reports negative absolute-inertia mass, normalized
Gram error and actual graph-distance errors. For unordered pairs \(i<j\), write
\(\widehat D_{ij}=\|y_i-y_j\|_2\):

$$
 E_G=\sqrt{\frac{\sum_{i<j}(\widehat D_{ij}-D_{G,ij})^2}
                       {\sum_{i<j}D_{G,ij}^2}},\qquad
 R_V=1-\operatorname{corr}(D_{G,ij},\widehat D_{ij})^2.
$$
\alttext(Relative geodesic error is the norm of the pair-distance errors divided by the graph-distance norm. Residual variance is one minus squared Pearson correlation of those distance vectors.)

Residual variance follows the original Isomap diagnostic. Correlation ignores affine
changes between the two distance vectors, so zero residual variance does not establish
an isometry. A constant distance vector has undefined correlation and is reported as NaN.
All of these scores describe the fitted graph; none can detect a wrong graph using only
its own shortest-path targets. `reference_diagnostics()` can compare with independent
reference coordinates when known, as on the Swiss roll below.

>> Computation and scope

Brute-force input distances cost \(O(n^2p)\), and deterministic partial sorting costs
\(O(n^2\log k)\). The dense shortest-path and symmetric eigendecomposition stages each
cost \(O(n^3)\). The implementation uses contiguous Floyd–Warshall matrices and a
Householder/implicit-QL eigensolver, with \(O(n^2+np+nk)\) storage. This is a dense
in-sample method; it has no out-of-sample transform or built-in validation procedure.
A small retained fraction or a high graph-fit score does not replace geometric checks.

> Simple examples

>> Four points on a line

Use \((x,y)=(0,0),(1,0),(2,0),(4,0)\) and \(k=2\). Directed neighbour lists are
\((1,2),(0,2),(1,0),(2,1)\), indexed from zero. In particular, the union graph has
edge \((1,3)\) even though row 1 did not select row 3. The only missing unordered
edge is \((0,3)\), whose shortest length is \(2+2=4\) through row 2.
Thus every graph distance is \(|x_i-x_j|\).

$$
 \bar x=\tfrac74,\qquad
 x_c=(-\tfrac74,-\tfrac34,\tfrac14,\tfrac94)^\top,\qquad
 B=x_cx_c^\top,\quad \lambda_1=x_c^\top x_c=\tfrac{35}4.
$$
\alttext(The four positions have mean seven quarters. Their centred outer product is the Gram matrix, whose only nonzero eigenvalue is thirty-five quarters. One embedding coordinate recovers those centred positions up to sign.)

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/isomap_four_points.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame d;d.add_column("x",std::vector<double>{0,1,2,4});d.add_column("y",std::vector<double>{0,0,0,0});
  const dm::stats::Isomap m(d,{"x","y"},{1,2});
  auto graph=m.neighborhood_graph().plot().title("Five union edges on four points").x_limits(-.5,4.5).y_limits(-.4,1.2);
  for(std::size_t i=0;i<4;++i)graph.text(d.double_at("x",i),.15,std::to_string(i));
  auto coordinate=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();coordinate.points(d.numeric_column("x").values(),m.dimension(0));
  coordinate.title("One axis recovers centred positions").x_label("original x").y_label("Isomap coordinate; arbitrary sign");
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(graph).add(coordinate).size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/isomap_four_points.svg");
  std::print("Union edges:\n\n{}\n\nPair distances:\n\n{}\n\nSpectrum:\n\n{}\n",
      m.neighborhood_graph().edge_table().to_md(),m.pair_distances().to_md(),m.spectrum().select({"dimension","eigenvalue"}).to_md(6));
}
```
