\caption(The square's graph metric is not a Euclidean square metric. Positive-inertia fit one and residual variance zero coexist with a 23.91% relative distance error.)
\alttext(The original four-cycle and its two-axis embedding are shown side by side. The table distinguishes unit graph edges from embedded square-root-two edges and reports the negative eigenvalue contribution.)

>> Two triangles are not one connected geometry

Take two unit right triangles ten units apart. At \(k=2\), each vertex selects the
other two vertices of its own triangle: there are six union edges and two components.
The cross-component block of \(D_G\) is infinite, so the constructor rejects a global
embedding. At \(k=3\), new long edges force the components together. That change makes
a computation possible; it does not establish that the gap represents a sampled surface.
The graph inspector allows both situations to be examined without catching a failed fit.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/isomap_components.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame d;d.add_column("x",std::vector<double>{0,1,0,10,11,10});d.add_column("y",std::vector<double>{0,0,1,0,0,1});
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2);dm::dstruct::DataFrame rows;
  for(std::size_t k:{2,3}){const auto graph=dm::stats::isomap_graph(d,{"x","y"},k);
    layout.add(graph.plot().title(std::format("k = {}: {} component{}",k,graph.component_sizes.size(),graph.component_sizes.size()==1?"":"s")).x_limits(-1,12).y_limits(-.3,2.3));
    const auto row=graph.diagnostics();rows=k==2?row:rows.concat_rows(row);}
  try{const dm::stats::Isomap rejected(d,{"x","y"},{2,2});}catch(const std::invalid_argument& e){std::print("Rejected fit: {}\n\n",e.what());}
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");layout.size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/isomap_components.svg");
  std::print("{}\n",rows.select({"k","components","edges","longest_edge"}).to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Rejected fit: Isomap: neighbor graph is disconnected (2 components); inspect isomap_graph() before choosing a neighborhood

| k | components | edges | longest_edge |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 2 | 2 | 6 | 1.414214 |
| 3 | 1 | 11 | 10.000000 |

\image(figures/isomap_components.svg)
// result_end

\caption(The graph at two neighbours has two independent triangles. Three neighbours introduce long bridges; the increased connectivity changes the metric rather than proving a manifold hypothesis.)
\alttext(Two panels show disconnected triangles with only short edges, then the same points connected by long cross-gap edges. The table reports component and edge counts.)

> Checking the theory on a Swiss roll

>> Known intrinsic coordinates give an independent target

`dm::datasets::swiss_roll(1000,0,1)` supplies a noiseless rolled sheet and withheld
coordinates. Points are \((t\cos t,h,t\sin t)\), where \(t\) ranges from
\(3\pi/2\) to \(9\pi/2\), and \(h\) from zero to 21. The true flat coordinate
along the spiral is arc length, not \(t\):

$$
 s(t)=\int_0^t\sqrt{1+u^2}\,du
      =\tfrac12\{t\sqrt{1+t^2}+\operatorname{asinh}t\},\qquad
 ds^2+dh^2=(1+t^2)dt^2+dh^2.
$$
\alttext(Arc length along the spiral integrates square root one plus t squared. Together with height it makes the rolled sheet's line element Euclidean.)

The intrinsic domain is a rectangle in \((s,h)\), so its geodesic distance is ordinary
Euclidean distance in those coordinates. Only `x,y,z` enter the fit. We evaluate complete
pair distances in \((s,h)\), making the check invariant to reflection or rotation of the
embedding. This avoids claiming recovery merely from one embedding axis correlating with
one latent parameter.

The two neighbourhoods below illustrate a local regime and a broad regime. They do not
establish a universal best \(k\). Colours use withheld arc length solely for interpretation.
`max_edge_stretch` is the largest intrinsic reference length of a selected edge divided
by its observed chord length; a large value exposes a shortcut when truth is available.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/isomap_swiss_maps.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto roll=dm::datasets::swiss_roll(1000,0,1);const auto color=dm::graphics::value_colors(roll.numeric_column("arc_length").values());
  auto observed=dm::graphics::RPlot::create(),truth=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  observed.points_colored(roll.numeric_column("x").values(),roll.numeric_column("z").values(),color,2.8).title("Observed roll, viewed along its axis").x_label("x").y_label("z");
  truth.points_colored(roll.numeric_column("arc_length").values(),roll.numeric_column("height").values(),color,2.8).title("Withheld flat coordinates").x_label("arc length s").y_label("height h");
  auto layout=dm::graphics::RLayout::create(2,2).add(observed).add(truth);dm::dstruct::DataFrame fits,references;
  for(std::size_t k:{6,40}){
    const dm::stats::Isomap m(roll,{"x","y","z"},{2,k});auto plot=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
    plot.points_colored(m.dimension(0),m.dimension(1),color,2.8).title(std::format("Isomap: k = {}",k)).x_label("Y1").y_label("Y2");layout.add(plot);
    auto fit=m.dimension_diagnostics().tail(1).select({"gof_positive","relative_geodesic_error","residual_variance"});fit.add_column("k",std::vector<double>{static_cast<double>(k)});
    fits=k==6?fit:fits.concat_rows(fit);auto reference=m.reference_diagnostics(roll,{"arc_length","height"});reference.add_column("k",std::vector<double>{static_cast<double>(k)});
    references=k==6?reference:references.concat_rows(reference);
  }
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");layout.size(1100,940).fit_panels().save_svg("build/isomap_swiss_maps.svg");
  std::print("Fit to each model's graph:\n\n{}\n\nIndependent intrinsic-distance checks:\n\n{}\n",
      fits.select({"k","gof_positive","relative_geodesic_error","residual_variance"}).to_md(6),
      references.select({"k","geodesic_relative_error","embedding_relative_error","max_edge_stretch"}).to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Fit to each model's graph:

| k | gof_positive | relative_geodesic_error | residual_variance |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 6 | 0.935099 | 0.022124 | 0.001271 |
| 40 | 0.534816 | 0.231600 | 0.253091 |


Independent intrinsic-distance checks:

| k | geodesic_relative_error | embedding_relative_error | max_edge_stretch |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 6 | 0.082408 | 0.074494 | 1.011531 |
| 40 | 0.605807 | 0.675332 | 11.024380 |

\image(figures/isomap_swiss_maps.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Observed roll, independent flat coordinates and two Isomap embeddings. Colours run from purple to yellow along increasing arc length. Graph-fit scores and independent intrinsic-distance errors answer different questions.)
\alttext(A coloured spiral and a flat coloured rectangle provide input and reference views. Below them are Isomap embeddings for six and forty neighbours, whose distortion is assessed in the accompanying tables.)

At six neighbours the graph-distance error to the true sheet is 8.24%, and the
embedding error is 7.45%. At forty neighbours these rise to 60.58% and 67.53%.
The maximum edge stretch rises from 1.01 to 11.02, directly identifying cross-fold
shortcuts rather than inferring them solely from an unattractive map.

>> A neighbourhood sweep and a density check

We inspect the graph first, then fit only connected cases. All candidates use the same
1000 points and the same two axes. The curves compare fidelity to the graph itself with
fidelity to the independently known sheet. Sparse graphs can detour and larger graphs
can short circuit; these are competing errors, so a monotone improvement is not expected.

A 500-point version with the same seed uses the first 500 noiseless sampled positions.
It illustrates that six neighbours can already create damaging shortcuts at lower sampling
density. Connectivity and the count \(k\) alone do not specify neighbourhood geometry.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/isomap_swiss_sweep.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto roll=dm::datasets::swiss_roll(1000,0,1);dm::dstruct::DataFrame graphs,errors;
  std::vector<double> connected_k,graph_error,intrinsic_error,inertia;
  for(std::size_t k:{3,4,6,8,12,20,40,80}){
    const auto graph=dm::stats::isomap_graph(roll,{"x","y","z"},k);const auto row=graph.diagnostics();graphs=k==3?row:graphs.concat_rows(row);
    if(graph.component_sizes.size()!=1)continue;
    const dm::stats::Isomap m(roll,{"x","y","z"},{2,k});const auto fit=m.dimension_diagnostics().tail(1),reference=m.reference_diagnostics(roll,{"arc_length","height"});
    auto error=reference.select({"geodesic_relative_error","embedding_relative_error","max_edge_stretch"});error.add_column("k",std::vector<double>{static_cast<double>(k)});
    errors=connected_k.empty()?error:errors.concat_rows(error);connected_k.push_back(k);graph_error.push_back(fit.double_at("relative_geodesic_error",0));
    intrinsic_error.push_back(reference.double_at("embedding_relative_error",0));inertia.push_back(m.goodness_of_fit());
  }
  auto metric=dm::graphics::RPlot::create(),scores=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  metric.lines(connected_k,graph_error,"to own graph",dm::RGB{37,99,235}).lines(connected_k,intrinsic_error,"to true sheet",dm::RGB{220,38,38});
  metric.title("Two different distance targets").x_label("neighbours k").y_label("relative distance error").y_limits(0,1.05);
  scores.lines(connected_k,inertia,"positive inertia",dm::RGB{37,99,235});scores.title("Positive-inertia fit is not truth").x_label("neighbours k").y_label("retained positive fraction").y_limits(0,1.15);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(metric).add(scores).size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/isomap_swiss_sweep.svg");
  const auto sparse=dm::datasets::swiss_roll(500,0,1);const dm::stats::Isomap small(sparse,{"x","y","z"},{2,6});
  std::print("Connectivity:\n\n{}\n\nIndependent errors:\n\n{}\n\n500 points, k=6:\n\n{}\n",graphs.select({"k","components","edges"}).to_md(),
      errors.select({"k","geodesic_relative_error","embedding_relative_error","max_edge_stretch"}).to_md(6),
      small.reference_diagnostics(sparse,{"arc_length","height"}).select({"n","geodesic_relative_error","embedding_relative_error","max_edge_stretch"}).to_md(6));
}
```
