\caption(Neighbourhood sensitivity on a fixed roll, with a separate lower-density case. Only connected candidates appear in the embedding curves; every candidate's component count is retained in the table.)
\alttext(Relative errors to graph and true-sheet distances are plotted against neighbour count. A second panel shows retained positive inertia. Tables include disconnected candidates and an independent 500-point check.)

The graph first connects at four neighbours in this sweep. Moving from six to eight
neighbours introduces an edge with intrinsic-to-chord ratio 10.94 and raises embedding
error from 7.45% to 32.08%. The 500-point case already has that shortcut at six neighbours,
with 33.16% embedding error. These are fixed-sample comparisons, not estimates of an
optimal neighbourhood for other samples.

> Iris measurements

>> Data and scaling

`dm::datasets::iris()` contains 150 flowers, four numeric measurements in centimetres and
a species label. We use all four measurements without scaling. Their shared physical unit
does not make their variances equal, but it makes the raw Euclidean baseline explicit.
Species is withheld from graph construction, fitting and diagnostics; it colours the final
map only. No classification accuracy or prediction on new flowers is estimated.

```{cpp, results="markdown", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto d=dm::datasets::iris();
  std::print("{} flowers; {} columns.\n\n{}\n",d.nrows(),d.ncols(),d | dm::group_by("Species") |
      dm::summarise(dm::agg::n("n"),dm::agg::mean("Sepal.Length","mean_sepal_length_cm"),dm::agg::mean("Petal.Length","mean_petal_length_cm"),dm::agg::mean("Petal.Width","mean_petal_width_cm")) | dm::to_md(3));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
150 flowers; 5 columns.

| Species | n | mean_sepal_length_cm | mean_petal_length_cm | mean_petal_width_cm |
| :--- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| setosa | 50 | 5.006 | 1.462 | 0.246 |
| versicolor | 50 | 5.936 | 4.260 | 1.326 |
| virginica | 50 | 6.588 | 5.552 | 2.026 |
// result_end

\caption(Species counts and measurements in centimetres. All selected features are complete, so retained and original row orders agree here.)

>> Connectivity is a property to inspect, not an automatic tuning rule

The next graph-only sweep does not fit invalid global maps. At small \(k\), separate
components are a genuine output. With the stated tie/union rule, iris first becomes
connected at \(k=25\). That is a fairly broad neighbourhood among 150 observations.
Joining separated groups is not proof that their shortest paths approximate travel on a
single biological manifold; component-wise analysis can be more defensible than forcing
a connection just to obtain one picture.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/isomap_iris_connectivity.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto d=dm::datasets::iris();const std::vector<std::string> features={"Sepal.Length","Sepal.Width","Petal.Length","Petal.Width"};
  dm::dstruct::DataFrame selected;std::vector<double> k,components,longest;
  for(std::size_t count=2;count<=30;++count){const auto graph=dm::stats::isomap_graph(d,features,count);const auto row=graph.diagnostics();
    k.push_back(count);components.push_back(graph.component_sizes.size());longest.push_back(row.double_at("longest_edge",0));
    if(count==2 || count==3 || count==10 || count==24 || count==25 || count==30)selected=selected.ncols()?selected.concat_rows(row):row;}
  auto connectivity=dm::graphics::RPlot::create(),length=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();
  connectivity.lines(k,components).points(k,components).title("Iris union-graph connectivity").x_label("neighbours k").y_label("connected components").y_limits(.5,4.5);
  length.lines(k,longest).title("Broader neighbourhoods add long edges").x_label("neighbours k").y_label("longest selected edge (cm metric)");
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(connectivity).add(length).size(1100,490).fit_panels().save_svg("build/isomap_iris_connectivity.svg");
  std::print("{}\n",selected.select({"k","components","edges","min_degree","max_degree","longest_edge"}).to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
| k | components | edges | min_degree | max_degree | longest_edge |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 2 | 4 | 211 | 2 | 7 | 0.883176 |
| 3 | 2 | 311 | 3 | 9 | 0.927362 |
| 10 | 2 | 987 | 10 | 21 | 1.389244 |
| 24 | 2 | 2242 | 24 | 45 | 1.951922 |
| 25 | 1 | 2321 | 25 | 47 | 1.997498 |
| 30 | 1 | 2760 | 30 | 59 | 2.102380 |

\image(figures/isomap_iris_connectivity.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Component counts and longest selected edge across the iris neighbourhood sweep. The first connected candidate is 25; graph degree can exceed k because adjacency uses the union rule.)
\alttext(Iris has four components at k equals two, two at k equals three through twenty-four, and one from twenty-five. The second panel shows increasing longest-edge length as neighbourhoods broaden.)

>> Compare a connected map with the Euclidean baseline

We display the \(k=25\) Isomap map beside two-dimensional classical Euclidean MDS on
the same unscaled features. Rotations and reflections are immaterial. The first table
checks Isomap against its own graph; the second checks both graph and embedded distances
against raw Euclidean feature distances. Those raw distances are a comparison baseline,
not known manifold truth as on the Swiss roll. A changed graph target must not be confused
with the MDS distance-reconstruction target.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/isomap_iris_maps.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
int main() {
  const auto d=dm::datasets::iris();const std::vector<std::string> features={"Sepal.Length","Sepal.Width","Petal.Length","Petal.Width"};
  const dm::stats::Isomap iso(d,features,{2,25});const dm::stats::MDS mds(d,features);
  std::vector<std::string> labels;for(auto row:iso.kept_row_indices())labels.push_back(d.string_at("Species",row));
  auto nonlinear=iso.plot_embedding(labels).title("Isomap, k = 25").y_limits(-2.5,4.0);
  auto baseline=mds.plot_embedding(labels).title("Euclidean classical MDS").y_limits(-2.5,4.0);
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(nonlinear).add(baseline).size(1100,510).fit_panels().save_svg("build/isomap_iris_maps.svg");
  std::print("Isomap versus its graph:\n\n{}\n\nIsomap versus original Euclidean distances:\n\n{}\n\nMDS versus original Euclidean distances:\n\n{}\n",
      iso.dimension_diagnostics().tail(1).select({"gof_positive","negative_mass","relative_geodesic_error","residual_variance"}).to_md(6),
      iso.reference_diagnostics(d,features).select({"geodesic_relative_error","embedding_relative_error"}).to_md(6),
      mds.dimension_diagnostics().tail(1).select({"gof_positive","relative_distance_error"}).to_md(6));
}
```
// result_begin: markdown
Isomap versus its graph:

| gof_positive | negative_mass | relative_geodesic_error | residual_variance |
| ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 0.944961 | 0.024390 | 0.063124 | 0.006956 |


Isomap versus original Euclidean distances:

| geodesic_relative_error | embedding_relative_error |
| ---: | ---: |
| 0.168406 | 0.171154 |


MDS versus original Euclidean distances:

| gof_positive | relative_distance_error |
| ---: | ---: |
| 0.977685 | 0.041796 |

\image(figures/isomap_iris_maps.svg)
// result_end

\caption(Two unsupervised iris maps coloured by held-out species labels. Similar visual separation does not establish a manifold model or supervised predictive performance; the distance targets and errors are reported separately.)
\alttext(Isomap and Euclidean MDS show the 150 flowers coloured by species. Tables separate Isomap's graph-fit diagnostics from both maps' errors relative to ordinary feature-space distances.)

The connected Isomap map has 6.31% relative error against its own graph, but 17.12%
against the original feature distances; MDS has 4.18% against those feature distances.
This comparison describes different targets and does not establish a biological winner.

An independent NumPy/SciPy calculation reproduces the stated iris graph, shortest paths,
spectrum and coordinate Gram matrix. Stock scikit-learn agrees on two continuous
Swiss-roll checks, but differs on iris: rounding and neighbour tie choices can change
the selected graph. Comparisons must match the graph before comparing coordinates.

> Conclusion

Isomap learns a global metric from local edges before it learns coordinates. The worked
examples separate shortest-path correctness, connectivity and Euclidean representability.
Even perfect positive-inertia fit and residual variance can hide distance distortion.
The Swiss roll supplies an independent target and exposes both neighbourhood and sampling
sensitivity. Iris illustrates a different limitation: obtaining connectivity by broadening
neighbourhoods does not validate a single connected manifold. Graph inspection, signed
spectra and clearly named distance targets make these distinctions reviewable.

> References

- Tenenbaum, J. B., de Silva, V. and Langford, J. C. (2000). [A Global Geometric Framework for Nonlinear Dimensionality Reduction|https://doi.org/10.1126/science.290.5500.2319]. ~Science~, 290(5500), 2319–2323. Isomap, geodesic approximation and residual variance.
- Torgerson, W. S. (1952). [Multidimensional scaling: I. Theory and method|https://doi.org/10.1007/BF02288916]. ~Psychometrika~, 17, 401–419. Classical scaling.
- scikit-learn developers. [Isomap|https://scikit-learn.org/stable/modules/generated/sklearn.manifold.Isomap.html] and [implementation|https://github.com/scikit-learn/scikit-learn/blob/main/sklearn/manifold/_isomap.py]. Connected-graph comparison on continuous Swiss-roll samples; iris neighbour ties and disconnected graphs are handled differently.
- Fisher, R. A. (1936). [The use of multiple measurements in taxonomic problems|https://doi.org/10.1111/j.1469-1809.1936.tb02137.x]. ~Annals of Eugenics~, 7(2), 179–188. Iris measurements, used directly through the built-in dataset.

